Novell's TUXEDO Enterprise TP System 5.0 for DEC OSF/1 V3.0

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Novell's TUXEDO Enterprise Transaction Processing System (ETPS) 5.0 has
   been ported to DEC OSF/1 V3.0 and will be sold and supported by Digital
   on a worldwide basis

o  TUXEDO ETPS 5.0 provides middleware and facilities for reliable computing
   for multi-platform DEC OSF/1 based small, medium, and large-scale
   client/server applications

o  Novell's TUXEDO ETPS 5.0 replaces DEC TUXEDO V2.1 for DEC OSF/1 software,
   and a no-charge migration package is now available

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Novell and Digital have signed an agreement for joint marketing and
technical development of Novell's TUXEDO ETPS 5.0. The TUXEDO ETPS 5.0 is a
high-performance framework for building business-critical client/server and
distributed transaction processing applications. TUXEDO 5.0 enables
developers to create applications that span multiple hardware platforms and
operating systems, with full freedom to mix and match those platforms to
best fit the application environment. The software is also ideal for
developers who need to rightsize applications to open systems from either
the PC-LAN environment or the mainframe online transaction processing (OLTP)
arena.

Interoperability with IBM Mainframe Systems

Interoperability with IBM mainframe systems can be achieved in two ways.
>From within a TUXEDO service routine, the DEC SNA APPC/LU6.2 Tool Kit for
DEC OSF/1 (SPD 62.67.xx) can be used to establish LU6.2 conversations with a
cooperating partner on an IBM mainframe. Synchronization levels 0 and 1 are
supported. Secondly, a TUXEDO service routine can use the DEC SNA High Level
Language API for DEC OSF/1 (SPD 62.70.xx). This API supports communications
using the LU2 protocol (3270 data stream) and is compatible with IBM's High
Level Language API. Full transactional interoperability using ISO TP
(ISO/IEC 10026) is under investigation.

BENEFITS

The TUXEDO 5.0 enhanced client/server model provides a clean and consistent
method for developing and configuring client/server applications. Modularity
is provided using a powerful, simple, and elegant API -- Application-to-
Transaction Manager Interface (ATMI). TUXEDO provides a general medium for
three-tier, client/server applications, and provides the developer with
access to a wide variety of popular 4GLs and tools.

TUXEDO features all of the classic capabilities associated with OLTP
applications:

o  Capacity for thousands of concurrent users
o  Large volumes of data
o  Short interactions
o  High transactional throughput
o  Predictable application inputs and outputs
o  Predictable, short response times
o  High data integrity and security
o  Concurent database access
o  High application availability
o  Manageable environment

The availability of TUXEDO 5.0 on a wide variety of UNIX platforms allows
easy deployment in multivendor environments and provides for a high-level of
application portability.

TUXEDO's /DCE option provides for integration with DCE; Novell's approach is
to provide DCE integration as an option for users. This approach enables
users to take advantage of the superior performance of TUXEDO software by
building applications directly on DEC OSF/1 when superior performance is
needed, or on DCE when DCE services are required. Novell is offering
interoperation with DCE based applications in TUXEDO 5.0; longer term,
TUXEDO ETPS will provide full, but optional integration with DCE.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING INFORMATION

TUXEDO ETPS 5.0 -- which includes development, runtime, and client
components -- is available now from Digital. All TUXEDO software ships with
CD-ROM documentation; users requiring hardcopy documentation sets need to
order them separately. TUXEDO software includes the DynaText browser for use
with a Windows/DOS PC. The next release of TUXEDO, scheduled for
availability in June 1995, will contain a version of DynaText for DEC OSF/1
software.

Licensing Information

o  Definition of concurrent users -- Concurrent Use is managed by the TUXEDO
   server by counting the number of clients that are logged into TUXEDO (for
   runtime or development). These users may or may not be processing "work;"
   nevertheless, they are counted as concurrent users if they are actively
   connected to TUXEDO. Time-outs can be deployed to automatically log off
   users who are inactive. The best approach is to purchase a license
   sufficient to handle the maximum number of users who should be logged on
   at any given time.

o  Runtime licenses -- Server licensing is based on the number of concurrent
   users in a TUXEDO domain and is not tied to a single server. As such, a
   TUXEDO Runtime license is a "floating license" that can cover multiple
   nodes in a TUXEDO configuration supporting a specific number of
   concurrent users, regardless of the node to which they are connected.
   TUXEDO ETMS does not require a separate Runtime license for each node.

o  Development licenses -- Development environment licensing is also based
   on concurrent access (same approach as for Runtime licenses).

o  Client software -- Each PC or desktop device requiring connectivity to
   TUXEDO servers requires a client software license. Note that clients are
   packaged with the server; for example, a 100 user server Runtime license
   includes 100 desktop clients.

