AlphaStudio Broadcast System for Entertainment Industry

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Will assure that programs are aired exactly as scheduled

o  Will record or play the highest quality video direct to and from disk
   with or without the option of compression

o  Will support a building block style hardware architecture and an open
   systems concept for the software architecture

o  Will maintain the original quality of program material even after
   numerous broadcasts or edits

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

The broadcast segment of the video market is experiencing explosive growth.
That growth is an indication of the major switch from using analog methods
to digital methods in all phases of broadcasting.

The AlphaStudio Broadcast System will be a complete broadcasting system free
from the labor and handling associated with analog tape environments.
Because the data will be digitally stored, the information will not degrade
as with the repeated use of analog tape. Disk-based video data eliminates
the need for constant searching, shuttle, or pre-roll. Management and
manipulation of the video data is realtime for true random access in a
non-linear operation. Broadcasters will be able to simply prepare, reorder,
and play out to air any video clip when it's needed, as if the video clips
were a collection of stills.

With Digital's adherence to open systems standards, the broadcaster is not
locked into a proprietary solution. This means applications are more
transportable and easier to share. Open systems allow video data to be
easily transferred and stored in a more universal format.

High Performance

The AlphaStudio Broadcast System will be a completely digital broadcast
video solution providing major functional advancements and economies of
operation compared to existing analog technology. Integrated high capacity,
high performance storage with high performance computing will provide
powerful full motion digital imaging on a true RISC based 64 bit computer
platform. The AlphaStudio Broadcast System will provide a state-of-the-art
solution to complex video data capture, editing, archiving, and accessing
needs in broadcast and post-production environments.

Users need all this performance for several reasons. D1 is a component
digital-video recording format that requires video data rates of 27 MB per
second, which conforms to CCIR-601, a definition of quality recommended by
the International Radio Consultative Committee. Digital's standard storage
products perform up to 30 MB per second with as few as 4 drives.
Reproduction of high quality video images require an enormous amount of
data. Digital's AlphaStudio Broadcast System is able to provide D1 video
data rate for a sustained period of time. Equally significant is the fact
that Digital has developed the solution using standard components.

The AlphaStudio Broadcast System will have the ability to be easily tailored
to fit each broadcaster's environment and specific requirements. Digital has
traditionally built modular systems using a building block approach with
standard components. Digital can build a complete broadcast system using the
basic building blocks from the AlphaStudio Broadcast System. This approach
will allow Digital to work with the user and grow the system without
discarding previously purchased equipment, providing true investment
protection.

A Customizable Solution

The AlphaStudio Broadcast System will be a tailorable packaged solution,
completely digital-based for a broadcast network or affiliate. This solution
will handle the high data rates required for the highest quality video --
D1, which is in use in today's broadcast stations. There are four main
subsystem areas of the AlphaStudio Broadcast System:

o  Automation control -- A communication system to control the flow of video
   material. Essential to the video information flow is the management and
   control of video transfers.

   Another key player in providing a complete solution to the user is Louth
   Automation. Louth's automation application is an easy-to-use, powerful
   tool to control the flow of video data. The user can observe frames
   representing several video clips before deciding which clip is needed and
   where to locate it. This interface is a key element in tracking and
   provides the functions necessary to manage the playing of video material.
   It helps ensure that programs are aired exactly when scheduled.

o  Broadcast REV (record, edit, view) system -- A key workstation that
   drives several broadcast applications, including commercial playback,
   digital video disk recording, and nonlinear editing

o  Library server -- A video archive storing thousands of hours of video
   material. Commercial clips, high-speed graphics, audiovisual edits,
   stills, or final film copies can be stored in the library. Video data can
   be input from outside sources directly into the AlphaStudio Library
   Server or into the  AlphaStudio broadcast REV system. Users will
   determine the storage target for incoming video data by the compression
   ratio of video data, the amount of video time needed to be stored, and
   the immediacy of retrieval for actual playback to air.

   The storage choices are directly related to these options and to the rate
   of speed the equipment can move the video data. For high-speed data
   requirements, a direct communication with the REV station CPU must be
   established with directly attached disk storage to match the data rate. A
   high quality standard is D1, requiring at least 27 MB per second to input
   or output this level of quality.

   Through Digital's relationship with Leitch Technologies, Inc., a video
   board was created to provide the unique capability to deliver an
   uncompressed D1 video stream. Digital's rackmount Alpha systems, combined
   with the high-speed IPI rackmount disk VELOCITOR system, comprise a
   powerful platform that can deliver this required performance. Digital can
   provide scalable choices of storage and computer components in the
   broadcast solution. Its workstations, servers, tape, optical, and disk
   storage products can deliver the performance and capacity to handle the
   video hours needed at the quality of video required.

o  Network backbone -- An architecture to efficiently distribute and manage
   video data. Digital's experience and product set in high performance
   networking will give users a reliable solution for the most demanding
   video transfer needs. Video transfers that are usually done by routers
   will be managed over the network or directly on the server without the
   associated performance problems.

   The video data can be protected by defining security groupings. FDDI, the
   network standard, allows video data transfers to move at 10 MB per
   second. This same performance can be multiplied over many locations
   without video data loss. For archiving video material, Digital has other
   cost effective standard Ethernet products for data transfers.

Implementation Services

Digital will assess studio requirements with the network or affiliate
stations. Then it will work with each individual studio to plan, design,
deliver, and implement any or all unique requirements to meet those needs.
Digital can plan, stage, and test the system prior to delivery.

Digital provides fast, trouble-free installations for critical applications
with the objective of minimizing downtime. If the broadcaster is faced with
a lengthy or complex implementation and cannot afford disruption, then
pre-staging is the answer.


